## How Roary Storage Works

Roary uses a shared parallel filesystem so the same files can be accessed
from the systems participating in your computational workflow.

<div class="storage-flow">

  <div class="storage-flow-step">

    <span>01</span>

    <div class="storage-flow-symbol">
      LOGIN
    </div>

    <strong>
      Login Environment
    </strong>

    <small>
      Prepare files
    </small>

  </div>


  <div class="storage-flow-arrow">
    ↔
  </div>


  <div class="storage-flow-step storage-flow-data">

    <span>02</span>

    <div class="storage-flow-symbol">
      LUSTRE
    </div>

    <strong>
      Shared Storage
    </strong>

    <small>
      /home and /scratch
    </small>

  </div>


  <div class="storage-flow-arrow">
    ↔
  </div>


  <div class="storage-flow-step">

    <span>03</span>

    <div class="storage-flow-symbol">
      NODE
    </div>

    <strong>
      Compute Nodes
    </strong>

    <small>
      Jobs access the same files
    </small>

  </div>

</div>

This means you normally do **not** need to manually copy your input files
from a login node to an individual compute node before running a Slurm job.

For example, if you create:

```text
/home/USERNAME/project/input.dat
```

from the login environment, a compute job can access the same path.


