## Storage Quotas

Storage on Roary is shared among many researchers. Quotas are used to
control how much space or how many files may be consumed.

A quota may apply to:

<div class="quota-type-grid">

  <div>

    <span>USER</span>

    <strong>
      Individual Usage
    </strong>

    <p>
      Storage associated with your HPC user.
    </p>

  </div>


  <div>

    <span>GROUP</span>

    <strong>
      Research Group Usage
    </strong>

    <p>
      Storage shared by members of a research allocation or group.
    </p>

  </div>


  <div>

    <span>SPACE</span>

    <strong>
      Capacity
    </strong>

    <p>
      Amount of filesystem space consumed.
    </p>

  </div>


  <div>

    <span>FILES</span>

    <strong>
      File Count
    </strong>

    <p>
      Number of files or filesystem objects consumed.
    </p>

  </div>

</div>

Quota values are not necessarily the same for every user or research group.


## Check Your Quota

The Roary login environment provides:

```bash
myquota
```

You can also use:

```bash
hpcusage
```

to review available and used HPC resources.

Depending on your allocation, `hpcusage` may show information for both
your individual user and the associated research group.


## Quota Is Different from Free Filesystem Space

These are two different concepts.

<div class="quota-vs-space">

  <div>

    <span>FILESYSTEM</span>

    <strong>
      Overall free space
    </strong>

    <code>
      df -h /home /scratch
    </code>

    <p>
      Shows the overall filesystem capacity visible to the system.
    </p>

  </div>


  <div>

    <span>QUOTA</span>

    <strong>
      What you are allowed to use
    </strong>

    <code>
      myquota
    </code>

    <p>
      Shows storage limits relevant to your account or allocation.
    </p>

  </div>

</div>

A filesystem can have free space remaining while your user or group has
already reached its quota.


## Check Directory Usage

Check the size of the current directory:

```bash
du -sh .
```

Check another directory:

```bash
du -sh DIRECTORY
```

Example:

```bash
du -sh ~/project
```

Show sizes of items in the current directory:

```bash
du -sh ./* 2>/dev/null | sort -h
```

!!! note "Large directory scans can be expensive"

    Commands such as `du` may need to examine many files.

    Avoid repeatedly scanning extremely large directories or directories
    containing millions of files.


## Check Filesystem Space

To see the overall filesystem view:

```bash
df -h /home /scratch
```

Because Roary uses Lustre, administrators or advanced users may also
encounter Lustre-specific commands.

For example:

```bash
lfs quota -u $USER -h /home
```

and:

```bash
lfs quota -u $USER -h /scratch
```

However, for normal user documentation, prefer:

```bash
myquota
```

or:

```bash
hpcusage
```

because quota enforcement may involve both user and research-group limits.


## Organizing Research Data

A clear directory layout makes jobs easier to reproduce and troubleshoot.

One example:

```text
project/
├── README.md
├── scripts/
├── input/
├── config/
├── logs/
├── results/
└── archive/
```

For a larger workflow:

```text
project/
├── raw_data/
├── reference/
├── scripts/
├── jobs/
├── logs/
├── intermediate/
└── results/
```

A useful rule is:

<div class="storage-rule-strip">

