### Example Slurm scratch pattern

The exact scratch directory convention for your research group may vary,
but a job can use a temporary directory based on its Slurm job ID.

For example:

```bash
#!/bin/bash

#SBATCH --job-name=scratch-example
#SBATCH --output=scratch_%j.out
#SBATCH --error=scratch_%j.err
#SBATCH --cpus-per-task=4
#SBATCH --mem=8G
#SBATCH --time=01:00:00

WORKDIR="/scratch/${USER}/${SLURM_JOB_ID}"

mkdir -p "$WORKDIR"

echo "Working directory: $WORKDIR"

# Stage input data
cp "$SLURM_SUBMIT_DIR/input.dat" "$WORKDIR/"

cd "$WORKDIR"

# Run your application here
# my_application input.dat > results.txt

# Copy retained results back
mkdir -p "$SLURM_SUBMIT_DIR/results"

cp -a results.txt "$SLURM_SUBMIT_DIR/results/" 2>/dev/null || true

# Remove temporary job data when appropriate
rm -rf "$WORKDIR"
```

!!! important

    This is an example workflow. Follow the scratch directory convention
    used by your research group if one has already been established.


## Data Transfer to Roary

Files can be transferred through SSH-based tools or the Open OnDemand
web interface.


### Upload a file with SCP

Run this from **your local computer**:

```bash
scp FILE USERNAME@hpclogin.fiu.edu:/home/USERNAME/
```

Example:

```bash
scp analysis.py abc123@hpclogin.fiu.edu:/home/abc123/
```


### Upload a directory

```bash
scp -r PROJECT_DIR USERNAME@hpclogin.fiu.edu:/home/USERNAME/
```


### Download a file from Roary

Run this from your local computer:

```bash
scp USERNAME@hpclogin.fiu.edu:/home/USERNAME/results.txt .
```


## Rsync

For large directory trees or transfers that may need to be repeated,
`rsync` is generally more convenient than repeatedly copying everything.

Upload:

```bash
rsync -avh --progress PROJECT/ \
USERNAME@hpclogin.fiu.edu:/home/USERNAME/PROJECT/
```

Download:

```bash
rsync -avh --progress \
USERNAME@hpclogin.fiu.edu:/home/USERNAME/results/ \
./results/
```

For an interrupted large transfer, retaining partial files can be useful:

```bash
rsync -avh --partial --progress SOURCE DESTINATION
```


