## File and Directory Permissions

Linux permissions determine who can read, modify, or access files.

Check a file:

```bash
ls -l FILE
```

Check a directory:

```bash
ls -ld DIRECTORY
```

Example:

```text
drwxr-x--- 2 username researchgroup 4096 Aug 27 12:00 project
```

The permission fields describe access for:

```text
USER     GROUP     OTHER
```

<div class="permission-grid">

  <div>
    <span>r</span>
    <strong>Read</strong>
  </div>

  <div>
    <span>w</span>
    <strong>Write</strong>
  </div>

  <div>
    <span>x</span>
    <strong>Execute / Enter</strong>
  </div>

</div>


### Add user write permission

```bash
chmod u+w FILE
```


### Add group read permission

```bash
chmod g+r FILE
```


### Add group read and write permission

```bash
chmod g+rw FILE
```


### Change the group when authorized

```bash
chgrp GROUP FILE
```

You must be a member of the target group.


<div class="permission-warning">

  <div>
    !
  </div>

  <section>

    <span class="roary-page-eyebrow">
      PERMISSIONS
    </span>

    <h3>
      Avoid using chmod 777
    </h3>

    <p>
      Giving every user full read, write, and execute permissions is
      usually unnecessary and can expose research data to unintended users.
    </p>

    <p>
      Use the minimum permissions required for your workflow.
    </p>

  </section>

</div>


## Shared Research Directories

Research groups may have shared directories that allow multiple authorized
users to work with the same project data.

Always verify the directory's group before changing permissions:

```bash
ls -ld DIRECTORY
```

If you cannot access a research-group directory that you should have access
to, do not attempt to bypass the permissions.

Email the HPC Admins:

[**hpcadmin@fiu.edu**](mailto:hpcadmin@fiu.edu)

Include:

- Your HPC username
- The directory path
- The research group or faculty account
- The complete permission error


## Managing Large Numbers of Files

HPC filesystems are optimized for large-scale parallel access, but very
large numbers of tiny files can create substantial metadata overhead.

Examples include:

```text
millions of JSON files
millions of tiny FASTQ fragments
thousands of temporary logs
millions of small model checkpoints
```

Potential symptoms include:

- Slow directory listings
- Slow `find` operations
- Slow copying
- Long cleanup times
- File-count quota exhaustion
- Increased metadata load


## Reduce Small-File Overhead

When appropriate, group files into archives.

For example:

```bash
tar -cf dataset.tar dataset/
```

Compress when useful:

```bash
tar -czf dataset.tar.gz dataset/
```

Inspect an archive without extracting:

```bash
tar -tf dataset.tar
```

Extract:

```bash
tar -xf dataset.tar
```

!!! tip

    Do not repeatedly compress and decompress very large datasets during
    active computation unless the workflow benefits from doing so.


## Avoid Expensive Filesystem Operations

Use caution with commands such as:

```bash
du -sh *
```

```bash
find /home -type f
```

```bash
ls -l
```

when operating on directories containing extremely large numbers of files.

Avoid searching the entire shared filesystem when you only need your own
project directory.

Prefer:

```bash
find ~/project -name "*.out"
```

instead of broad filesystem-wide searches.


## Job Output Files

Slurm output files can accumulate quickly.

A typical script may contain:

```bash
#SBATCH --output=logs/job_%j.out
#SBATCH --error=logs/job_%j.err
```

Create the log directory before submission:

```bash
mkdir -p logs
```

Using `%j` prevents jobs from overwriting one another because it expands
to the Slurm job ID.


## Keep Logs Organized

For large projects:

```text
project/
├── jobs/
│   ├── analysis.slurm
│   └── preprocessing.slurm
│
├── logs/
│   ├── analysis_123456.out
│   ├── analysis_123456.err
│   ├── preprocessing_123457.out
│   └── preprocessing_123457.err
│
└── results/
```

This makes troubleshooting considerably easier.


## Temporary Application Files

Many scientific applications create temporary files.

When possible, point large temporary output to an appropriate scratch
working directory instead of filling your primary project location.

Applications may use environment variables such as:

```bash
TMPDIR
```

For workflows that support it, you can set:

```bash
export TMPDIR="/scratch/${USER}/${SLURM_JOB_ID}"
mkdir -p "$TMPDIR"
```

Always confirm that the application honors `TMPDIR` before relying on it.


## Check for Large Files

Find files larger than 10 GB inside the current project:

```bash
find . -type f -size +10G -ls
```

Show the largest top-level items:

```bash
du -sh ./* 2>/dev/null | sort -h
```

For very large projects, run these checks selectively rather than repeatedly.


## Find Old Files

Example:

```bash
find . -type f -mtime +30 -print
```

This lists files whose modification time is more than 30 days old.

!!! warning

    Do not automatically delete files based only on age unless you
    understand the project and retention requirements.


## Clean Up After Jobs

A completed computational job may leave behind:

- Temporary input copies
- Intermediate data
- Checkpoints
- Temporary archives
- Debug logs
- Unused output
- Failed-run directories

Review and remove data that is no longer required.

Before deleting:

```bash
du -sh DIRECTORY
```

and:

```bash
ls DIRECTORY
```

Then remove only the intended data.


## Removing Files

Delete one file:

```bash
rm FILE
```

Delete an empty directory:

```bash
rmdir DIRECTORY
```

Delete a directory recursively:

```bash
rm -r DIRECTORY
```

