    <strong>
      No space left on device
    </strong>

    <p>
      The relevant filesystem, allocation, or quota may not have enough
      available capacity.
    </p>

  </div>


  <div>

    <span>PERMISSION</span>

    <strong>
      Permission denied
    </strong>

    <p>
      Your user or group does not currently have the required access.
    </p>

  </div>


  <div>

    <span>FILES</span>

    <strong>
      Too many files
    </strong>

    <p>
      A file-count quota or very large number of small files may be involved.
    </p>

  </div>


  <div>

    <span>SCRATCH</span>

    <strong>
      Missing scratch data
    </strong>

    <p>
      Scratch files are temporary and may have been removed under the
      automatic purge policy.
    </p>

  </div>


  <div>

    <span>I/O</span>

    <strong>
      Slow filesystem operations
    </strong>

    <p>
      Large directory scans, large numbers of small files, or storage
      activity from many jobs can affect performance.
    </p>

  </div>

</div>


## Troubleshooting Storage

Start with:

```bash
pwd
```

```bash
ls -ld .
```

```bash
myquota
```

```bash
hpcusage
```

```bash
df -h /home /scratch
```

For a specific directory:

```bash
du -sh DIRECTORY
```

Check ownership and permissions:

```bash
ls -ld DIRECTORY
```

If a job is involved, also keep the Slurm job ID.


## When Contacting the HPC Admins

Email:

[**hpcadmin@fiu.edu**](mailto:hpcadmin@fiu.edu)

Include:

- Your HPC username
- The exact filesystem or directory path
- Whether the issue involves `/home` or `/scratch`
- The research group involved, if applicable
- The exact command you ran
- The complete error message
- The Slurm job ID, if the problem occurred during a job

Never include your password.


## Storage Rules to Remember

<div class="storage-final-rules">

  <div>
    <span>01</span>
    <strong>/home and /scratch are shared filesystems.</strong>
  </div>

  <div>
    <span>02</span>
    <strong>Do not treat Roary storage as your only backup.</strong>
  </div>

  <div>
    <span>03</span>
    <strong>/scratch files are automatically removed after 30 days.</strong>
  </div>

  <div>
    <span>04</span>
    <strong>Check your quota before large workflows.</strong>
  </div>

  <div>
    <span>05</span>
    <strong>Use scratch for temporary, reproducible job data.</strong>
  </div>

  <div>
    <span>06</span>
    <strong>Move important results out of scratch.</strong>
  </div>

  <div>
    <span>07</span>
    <strong>Avoid millions of unnecessary small files.</strong>
  </div>

  <div>
    <span>08</span>
    <strong>Use group permissions carefully.</strong>
  </div>

  <div>
    <span>09</span>
    <strong>Never use chmod 777 as a routine solution.</strong>
  </div>

  <div>
    <span>10</span>
    <strong>Back up irreplaceable research data elsewhere.</strong>
  </div>

</div>


